<h2>What to wear to après-ski (and the après-ski look)</h2>
<p>Most people show up straight from the slopes, so ski clothes are completely normal. If you want to change, the classic après look is warm and cozy rather than fancy:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Insulated winter boots.</strong> The single best upgrade. Grippy, warm boots like <%= gear_link "Sorel 1964 Pac boots", "Sorel 1964 Pac boots" %> or other <%= gear_link "insulated winter boots", "insulated winter boots" %> handle icy decks and village paths. More picks in <%= link_to "best snow boots", guide_path("best-snow-boots") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>A chunky sweater or fleece.</strong> A <%= gear_link "wool ski sweater", "wool ski sweater" %> or a <%= gear_link "sherpa fleece", "sherpa fleece pullover" %> is the classic après layer.</li>
  <li><strong>A puffy or vest on top.</strong> Easy to pull off indoors. A <%= gear_link "down vest", "down vest" %> keeps your core warm on an outdoor deck.</li>
  <li><strong>A beanie.</strong> It fixes helmet hair and keeps you warm outside.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're staying in ski clothes, these tips make it more comfortable:</p>
<h3>Coming straight off the slopes</h3>
<p>Most people show up straight from the slopes, so ski clothes are completely normal. A few tips make it more comfortable:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Swap your boots.</strong> Walking in ski boots is clumsy and tiring. If you can, change into warm, grippy winter boots before heading to the deck or into town.</li>
  <li><strong>Unzip and layer down.</strong> Indoors gets warm fast. A base layer and a light mid layer under your jacket let you cool off. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> for how layers work.</li>
  <li><strong>Bring a warm hat.</strong> Helmet hair is universal. A beanie fixes it and keeps you warm outside.</li>
  <li><strong>Dry socks help.</strong> A fresh pair in your bag makes a big difference after a wet day. Add it to your <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Après-ski etiquette</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Store your gear properly.</strong> Use ski racks, and don't lean skis or boards against doorways or block paths.</li>
  <li><strong>Be mindful of space.</strong> Decks and lodges get crowded at last chair. Share tables and don't spread gear across benches.</li>
  <li><strong>Tip your servers and bartenders.</strong> Ski-town staff work long, busy seasons.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep it friendly.</strong> Après is relaxed and social. Strangers chatting about the day is part of the fun.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Staying safe</h2>
<p>Après is meant to be fun, so plan ahead:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Don't drink and drive.</strong> Mountain roads are steep, dark, and often icy. Use resort shuttles, a ride service, walk to your lodging, or pick a designated driver.</li>
  <li><strong>Altitude adds up.</strong> At high-elevation resorts, alcohol can hit harder and dehydration comes on faster. Drink water between drinks.</li>
  <li><strong>Be done skiing first.</strong> Après comes after your last run, not between runs. Skiing impaired puts you and others at risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch the walk home.</strong> Parking lots and village paths freeze after sunset. Good boots and a headlamp or phone light help.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Making the most of your ski day</h2>
<p>The best après follows a great day on the snow. If you're new to the sport, start with <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> so you finish the day tired and happy instead of sore and frustrated. Then enjoy the part of skiing everyone agrees on.</p>
